/// A type name may be qualified by the unit that exports it.
///
/// A qualified name already works wherever a VALUE is expected -- Shapes.Circle()
/// constructs today -- and nowhere a TYPE is expected.  Both of these are
/// refused:
///
///     var C : Shapes.Circle := Shapes.Circle ();
///     WriteLn (Str (C is Shapes.Circle));
///
/// ⚠️ The gap matters more than the inconsistency does.  Two units exporting one
/// name is refused outright -- ''Circle'' is already defined; mark it private in
/// one of the modules.' -- and the advice there is to AVOID the collision rather
/// than resolve it.  A qualified type name is the mechanism that would resolve
/// it, so this is the smaller half of a larger gap.
///
/// Fails with: Expect ';' after variable declaration.  (file rejected -- no test ran)
///
uses 'lib/Shapes';

test 'A type name may be qualified by its unit';
begin
    var Round : Shapes.Circle := Shapes.Circle ();

    AssertEqual (3, Round.Area ());
    AssertTrue  (Round is Shapes.Circle);
end
